<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.JwtBearer" Version="10.0.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi" Version="10.0.10" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.OpenApi" Version="2.3.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Scalar.AspNetCore" Version="2.0.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Serilog.AspNetCore" Version="9.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Maren.Application\Maren.Application.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Maren.Persistence\Maren.Persistence.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Maren.Infrastructure\Maren.Infrastructure.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Maren.Contracts\Maren.Contracts.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    GHSA-v5pm-xwqc-g5wc — Microsoft.OpenApi, high severity.

    A patched line now EXISTS and we cannot take it yet. Verified 2026-08-01,
    not assumed: Microsoft.OpenApi 3.9.0 clears the advisory outright. The
    vulnerable-package scan (with transitives included) reports zero across all
    eight projects. But the build then fails with two CS0200s:

        OpenApiXmlCommentSupport.generated.cs(399,41): error CS0200:
        Property or indexer 'IOpenApiMediaType.Example' cannot be assigned
        to, because it is read only

    That file is emitted by Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi 10.0.10's own source
    generator, which is written against the 2.x contract where `Example` had a
    setter. So the blocker is the framework package, not the advisory, and no
    change in this repository can clear it.

    REVISIT TRIGGER: when Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi ships a version built
    against Microsoft.OpenApi 3.x, take it and DELETE this whole block. The
    check is one command — bump to 3.9.0 and build.

    The sentence this replaced said "no patched version" and "the advisory
    covers the whole 2.x line, so upgrading does not clear it". Both were true
    when written on 2026-07-22 and the first is now false; it was believed for
    ten days because nobody re-ran the search. An accepted risk needs its
    evidence re-checked, not just its reasoning re-read.

    Accepted risk, reviewed 2026-07-22. Reachability assessed rather than
    assumed: Microsoft.OpenApi is a transitive dependency of
    Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi, and the only code that exercises it is
    MapOpenApi() / MapScalarApiReference(), both of which sit inside
    `if (app.Environment.IsDevelopment())` in Program.cs. No production request
    reaches the vulnerable path.

    Pinned to the newest 2.x so any OTHER fix is picked up; the advisory covers
    the whole 2.x line, so upgrading does not clear it.

    Suppressed here rather than by disabling NuGetAudit, so every other
    advisory still fails the build. Recorded in docs/SECURITY_RISKS.md with a
    revisit trigger: remove this the moment a patched version ships, or the day
    OpenAPI is exposed outside Development.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-v5pm-xwqc-g5wc" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
